\section*{Positive consistency of Naimark's problem and a local hereditary reduction}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
Within ZFC, the paper claims an infinite-dimensional simple unital C*-algebra carrying a faithful trace but only one unitary-equivalence class of nonzero irreducible representations, providing another counterexample to Naimark's problem. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 297, 2026-09-24): Reduced HNN adjunctions join selected pure-state classes using matching peaking projections while preserving unique extensions of all old pure states. Reduced-word estimates prevent unintended identifications. Dependency-closed subiterations ensure countable determination, so a recursion of length continuum-successor eventually catches every pure state without a diamond prediction principle.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 297, 2026-09-24): The ZFC-constructed infinite-dimensional unital simple C*-algebra has all nonzero irreducible representations equivalent but is not K(H), refuting Naimark’s universal statement and therefore its proposed positive relative consistency; classical separable uniqueness forces this example to be nonseparable.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 297, the scope page documents the following selections. The selected ZFC construction has a faithful trace and a unique equivalence class of irreducible representations, but is a simple infinite-dimensional unital algebra rather than a compact-operator algebra. The documented statement covers the main claim of manuscript 1. Its selected declaration also covers the clause used for this problem. Comparator files: Naimark.lean. Declarations: OAI.NaimarkZFC.main.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

